{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 e6 4. O-O Be7 5. d3 O-O 6. Nbd2 c5 7. e4 Nc6 8. Re1 { Authored anchor. Re1 supports e4 and makes e5 more credible. Black has a broad d5-c5 center and may expand on the queenside while White builds a kingside initiative. } 8... Qc7 9. c3 h6 10. Qe2 Rd8 { Plan: Check whether e5 is safe, then consider Nf1 and h4 only if the center is stable. White's queen bishop needs a route, often through f4 or g5 after development. Opponent resource: Black can play ...Qc7, ...b5 or ...dxe4. A kingside attack is premature if e4 can simply be captured. } *

