{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 e6 4. O-O Be7 5. d3 O-O 6. Nbd2 c5 7. e4 d4 { Authored anchor. Black gains space and closes the d-file. White must choose where to challenge the chain rather than follow a standard e5 attack by habit. } 8. a4 b6 9. e5 Nd5 10. Nc4 Nc6 { Plan: Consider c3 against d4, or Nc4 if the square is available. Keep the f-pawn flexible until you know whether kingside expansion is safe. Opponent resource: ...Nc6 and ...e5 can consolidate a space advantage. Waiting without a pawn break can leave every white piece short of squares. } *

