{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 c6 4. O-O Bf5 5. d3 e6 6. Nbd2 { Authored anchor. Black develops the light-squared bishop before closing it with ...e6. White cannot assume that Black has the restricted bishop of a French-type position. } 6... h6 7. c4 Be7 8. b4 O-O 9. Qb3 Nbd7 10. Bb2 a5 { Plan: Prepare e4 with adequate support, or choose c4 to challenge d5 directly. Consider Nh4 only if it wins a useful bishop exchange without neglecting the center. Opponent resource: The bishop on f5 influences e4 and may move to g6 if chased. A knight trip can cost more time than the bishop is worth. } *

