{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Bg4 3. Bg2 Nd7 4. O-O e6 5. d3 Ngf6 6. Nbd2 { Authored anchor. Black develops actively while retaining the c-pawn's choice between c5 and c6. White keeps the usual pieces but must watch central pressure. } 6... Bh5 7. h3 h6 8. Qe1 Be7 9. e4 O-O 10. Nh4 dxe4 { Plan: Use e4 with sufficient support and prepare Re1 if needed. The queen can sometimes unpin Nf3 by moving from d1, but move it only for a clear reason. Opponent resource: Copying the Slav plan can be inaccurate because Black can play ...c5 in one move. White's d3 pawn can become a target after central exchanges. } *

