{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. g3 g6 3. Bg2 Bg7 4. O-O O-O 5. d3 d6 6. Nbd2 e5 7. e4 { Authored anchor. Both sides have fianchettoed and established restrained centers. White's extra move is useful, but the standard French-style kingside attack has no obvious hook. } 7... c5 8. a4 Nc6 9. Nc4 h6 10. Ne3 Be6 { Plan: Improve pieces and decide between c3-d4, c4 or a restrained queenside expansion. Compare the bishops' diagonals before exchanging central pawns. Opponent resource: An unnecessary e5 can be captured or leave d5 weak. Black can challenge the center with ...c5 just as White can with c4. } *

