{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. g3 g6 3. Bg2 Bg7 4. O-O O-O 5. d3 d5 6. Nbd2 Nc6 7. e4 e5 { Authored anchor. Black claims a broad center. White may need a central exchange or a lever rather than another slow redeployment. } 8. a4 a5 9. c3 Be6 10. Re1 d4 { Plan: Consider exd5 and Re1, checking the recapture and pressure against e5. Look for c4 or c3-d4 only after comparing the resulting weak squares. Opponent resource: Black can advance ...d4 and squeeze the knights. Passivity is a practical risk even without a direct tactical threat. } *

