{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 g6 3. Bg2 Bg7 4. O-O e5 5. d3 Ne7 6. Nbd2 O-O 7. e4 { Authored anchor. Black develops the king knight to e7 and holds d5-e5. The absence of a knight on f6 changes both e5 ideas and possible kingside exchanges. } 7... a5 8. a4 Re8 9. c3 Nbc6 10. Re1 d4 { Plan: Use a central lever and complete development. Consider exd5 when the resulting open file can be used by a rook rather than merely exchanging for its own sake. Opponent resource: Black may play ...Nbc6 or ...c5, gaining more space. Do not waste a tempo preparing to displace a knight that is not on f6. } *

