{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. g3 b5 3. Bg2 Bb7 4. O-O e6 5. d3 Be7 6. e4 { Authored anchor. Black gains queenside space and develops the bishop to b7. White uses the time to establish e4 but must notice the long diagonal toward the king side. } 6... d6 7. a4 b4 8. a5 c5 9. c3 O-O 10. Re1 Na6 { Plan: Develop Nbd2 and support the center. a4 may question b5 if it creates a useful weakness without costing a central tempo. Opponent resource: Black can pressure e4 after ...d5 or ...c5. A flank pawn advance may be part of central control, not merely a waste of time. } *

